Interface vs Abstract class
---------------------------
Feature                     Interface                                                                       Abstract Class
Purpose                     Defines a contract (what to do).                                                Provides a partial implementation (how to do).
Implementation              Can only have method signatures (and since Java 8 — default & static methods).  Can have both abstract and concrete methods.
State (fields)              Only public static final constants (no instance state).                         Can have instance variables, constructors, and state.
Inheritance                 A class can implement multiple interfaces.                                      A class can extend only one abstract class.
Constructors                Cannot have constructors.                                                       Can have constructors.
Access modifiers            All methods are implicitly public abstract (except default / static).           Can have any access modifiers.
Version                     Introduced since Java 1.0; evolved with Java 8–17 for flexibility.              Introduced in Java 1.0


Use an Interface when…                                                                              Use an Abstract Class when…
You just need to define a contract that multiple unrelated classes can implement                    You have closely related classes that share code, common state, or utility methods.
(e.g., Comparable, Runnable).
You want to achieve multiple inheritance of type (a class can implement multiple interfaces).       You need to provide default behavior, but still require subclasses to fill in specific parts.
The behavior is independent of implementation details (e.g., logging, persistence, analytics).      The behavior shares data or helper methods (e.g., base HTTP handler with shared connection logic).

🧠 2. How interfaces achieve decoupling
Interfaces separate the contract (what to do) from the implementation (how it’s done).
Instead of one class depending on another’s concrete type, it depends on an abstraction — the interface.

